course: dictionary
lesson: 6440
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one you need. «A rusty nail actually weighs more than the clean one» is about a thin metal pin, because only a metal pin can go rusty. «Fingernails are cut following their curve» is about the hard parts at the ends of your fingers. Read the words around the word first.
explain: Nail can also be a doing word. «To nail something» means to fix it with nails, as when you hit nails into a board. In everyday talk, «She nailed the test» is informal and means she did it perfectly. Ask yourself: is nail a thing or an action here, and does your meaning make sense in the sentence?
explain: Some phrases use nail too. «Hit the nail on the head» means you are exactly right, and «as hard as nails» means very tough. You cannot work out a phrase one word at a time, so learn it as a group of words.
fact: A nail can be a thin, pointed piece of metal that you hit with a hammer to join pieces of wood.
fact: Your fingernails and toenails are nails too: the hard parts at the ends of your fingers and toes.
fact: To nail something means to fix it with nails. «She nailed the test» means she did it perfectly.
fact: «Hit the nail on the head» means you are exactly right.
fact: «The parts you cut, hair and nails, are not alive», which is why cutting them never hurts.
exercise: «Fingernails are cut following their curve.» In this sentence, what is a nail? | To fix something with nails | *The hard part at the end of your finger | To do something perfectly | A thin metal pin for wood
exercise: «She nailed the test.» What does nailed mean here? | Fixed it to the wall with nails | *Did it perfectly | Bit it hard | Cut it straight across
exercise: What does the phrase «hit the nail on the head» mean? | You are very tired | You hit a nail with a hammer by accident | You bite your nails when you are worried | *You are exactly right
test: What does nail mean in «He used a hammer to nail the planks together»? | To do something perfectly | The hard part at the end of a toe | *To fix something with nails
test: What does nail mean in «The rusty nail weighed more than the clean one»? | To cut something straight across | The hard part at the end of your finger | *A thin, pointed piece of metal
exam: Which sentence uses nail as a verb meaning to do something perfectly? | «Hit the nail with the hammer.» | «Trim your nails once a week.» | *«She nailed the spelling test.»
